    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok i see. so even if i lower the ttl now, there are still places where it will hang onto the old info? it just feels so random, like some parts of the web update instantly and others stay stuck on the old stuff for hours for no apparent reason. is there anything else that causes that kind of inconsistency?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, even if you lower the TTL now, there are still places where it will hang onto the old info because the TTL is only a suggestion to the caching servers; some of them are stubborn, while others are more compliant, and some are just running on a different schedule. It feels random because the internet is a decentralized mess of thousands of independent caches, some of which are run by well-meaning but slow-downloaded servers, while others are aggressively caching for performance.\n\nTo understand why it's so inconsistent, you have to look at the hierarchy of DNS resolvers. When a user queries a name, their ISP might have a local cache, their corporate firewall might have a corporate cache, and the root servers might have their own. If your client's site is hosted on a server that has a very high TTL (like 86400 seconds, which is a full day), then every single one of those caches will hold onto the old IP for 24 hours, regardless of what you do now.\n\nHowever, there are other factors beyond just the TTL that cause this inconsistency. For example, some DNS providers use \"anycast\" networks, which means the same IP address is served from different physical locations around the",
      "source": "target",
